# TEST FIXTURE — served by the dnsmasq container that
# `./run.py up --test-policy` starts, and read only through it. Never
# present in normal operation.
#
# The records come from `[fixture.records]` in config.toml, which is also
# what `[policy.test].allow` is checked against: a record the test policy
# does not allowlist, or an allowlisted `.test` name with no record behind
# it, fails to render rather than turning into a check that silently grades
# nothing (docs/policy.md, "Test policy").
#
# Why this exists: `dns-mixed-answers` asks whether an engine that resolves
# a name to *several* addresses validates all of them, or silently picks
# the public one and connects. No public wildcard-DNS service serves a
# mixed answer set, so it has to be manufactured locally.
#
# Why it is not simply bind-mounted at /etc/hosts: it would not work.
# Duplicate names in a hosts file are collapsed to a single address by both
# resolvers involved — musl's `getent hosts` returns the first, Squid's own
# hosts parser keeps the last — so the engine never sees more than one
# address and the check would measure which record survived rather than how
# a multi-address answer is handled. dnsmasq's `--addn-hosts` aggregates
# them properly and returns both in one answer (verified 2026-08-25).
#
# The names are under `.test`, a reserved TLD (RFC 6761) that can never
# resolve publicly, so a missing fixture degrades to NXDOMAIN rather than
# to some real host.
#
# The public half accepts TCP on 443, so an engine that wrongly allowed one
# of the mixed names actually establishes a tunnel and is caught, rather
# than failing for lack of a route and looking like a denial. It is
# deliberately not the address `direct-ip-connect` uses: dnsmasq
# synthesizes PTR records from this file, and Squid's `dstdomain` falls
# back to a reverse lookup when the destination is an IP literal and
# nothing else matched — so a shared address let a bare-IP CONNECT through
# the allowlist under a fixture name and turned `direct-ip-connect` into a
# failure (measured 2026-08-25). config.toml refuses to render a fixture
# that reuses `ptr_address`. The Squid behavior it exposed is real and is
# written up in docs/engines.md, "Squid: a bare IP could satisfy the
# allowlist through a reverse lookup".
#
# `{{ fixture.rebind_zone }}` is NOT here: its answers change between the
# first lookup and the next, which no static file can express, so
# images/dnsfixture/rebind.py serves that zone instead.
{% for record in fixture_records %}

{% if record.role == "control" %}
# Control: one public address, nothing else. It must be reachable through
# the proxy for the check to mean anything — if this is denied, the fixture
# is not running, not allowlisted, or unreachable, and the mixed results
# below could not be attributed to mixed-answer handling. checks/egress.py
# skips on that rather than banking a pass it did not earn.
{% else %}
{% if loop.first or fixture_records[loop.index0 - 1].role == "control" %}
# The mixed answers. Every ordering below is present because an engine that
# validates only the first address returned would pass one and fail the
# other, and a single ordering could not tell those apart.
#
{% endif %}
# {{ record.shape }}.
{% endif %}
{% for address in record.addresses %}
{{ "%-15s"|format(address) }} {{ record.name }}
{% endfor %}
{% endfor %}
